Whether a patient is worth trialling a dopamine agonist — and how to confirm the drug reached its receptor — both lack a cheap test: every current dopaminergic measurement in this paper is a research scan (VMAT2 PET, D2 occupancy). Two peripheral observations converge on a blood-based candidate.

#speculation(title: [Elevated Baseline Prolactin (± Low Fasting ACTH) May Enrich for Dopaminergic-Agonist Response; the Pramipexole-Induced Prolactin Fall Is a Cheap Engagement Proxy])[
*Certainty: 0.25.* (cross-disease anchor n = 5 + lactotroph pharmacology; no ME/CFS-specific data)

Prolactin is tonically inhibited by tuberoinfundibular dopamine acting on lactotroph D2 receptors, and a D2/D3 agonist such as pramipexole suppresses it — so the prolactin response to the drug is a peripherally readable engagement signal, of the hypothalamic/pituitary (lactotroph) D2 compartment only, the same compartment the buspirone challenge reads out (@spec:prolactin-dopamine-window): the peripheral (lactotroph) analogue of the central (ventral-striatal) PET target-engagement readout in @Ventorp2026PramipexoleAnhedonia. At 0.5 mg the lactotroph fall is expected pharmacology in any patient, so the information content is in the within-patient trend (magnitude and timing against the patient's own baseline), not the mere fact of a fall. The responder-anchoring observation: in the placebo-controlled bromocriptine crossover in 5 post-polio fatigue patients, the 3 responders differed from the 2 non-responders in having ~2× plasma prolactin and low fasting ACTH, with more MRI hyperintensities and impaired attention @Bruno1996BromocriptinePostPolio. Elevated baseline prolactin would then mark patients whose tonic dopaminergic inhibition of prolactin is already reduced — a deficiency state an agonist would correct (the pituitary→CNS generalization is asserted, not derived). Convergence within this paper: ME/CFS patients mount an *exaggerated* prolactin response to a buspirone (a 5-HT1A agonist with D2 blockade) challenge despite normal baseline @cf:prolactin-buspirone-exaggerated — the mechanism of that response is four-way open in this paper (dopaminergic, 5-HT1A, estradiol, or nausea-driven, @oq:prolactin-buspirone-mechanism; the dopaminergic account is constrained-in, not confirmed) — the same readout, a different ligand, pointing at a dopaminergic-tone abnormality in ME/CFS itself if the dopaminergic account is the right one. The paper's prolactin line independently proposes that challenge response as a *subtype* marker (@spec:prolactin-dopamine-window, @spec:prolactin-subtype-marker). Read together, the two are two readouts of the same compartment offered as one claim; whether they mark one subgroup requires a bridging premise the data do not yet establish — the buspirone line's ME/CFS patients had normal baseline prolactin, so an elevated-baseline enrichment screen enrols almost none of that line's patients, and the enrichment claim needs its own ME/CFS baseline distribution. The transferable half is the engagement fall; the enrichment half is untested, and the in-paper baseline data argue against a direct transfer.

*Competing explanations:* (a) Prolactin is non-specifically raised by stress, thyroid disease, and multiple co-prescriptions (antipsychotics, metoclopramide) — without a controlled co-medication review, "elevated" is uninterpretable; these agents are standard on the ME/CFS chart. (b) Bruno's ACTH component implies an HPA-axis marker as much as a dopaminergic one; the n = 5 pilot cannot separate them, and the authors themselves called for multicentre confirmation. (c) The ME/CFS buspirone-prolactin data are pre-2010 (pre-Fukuda criteria, small, with a nausea confound not separable in that design — see its own limitation note). (d) The emetic/stress reflex: nausea raised by the agonist itself raises prolactin (the "most parsimonious alternative" recorded at @oq:prolactin-buspirone-mechanism) — a within-assay confound that can mask or mimic a dose-dependent fall. (e) The readout inherits the supersensitivity premise's challenge: normal CSF DA/DOPAC/HVA with selective noradrenergic deficiency in post-infectious ME/CFS @Aregawi2026Noradrenergic — without a chronic tonic DA deficit, the "deficiency state an agonist would correct" has no documented ME/CFS referent.

*Falsifiable prediction.* In any fatigue-syndrome cohort trialling a D2/D3 agonist: (1) baseline plasma prolactin above the reference range enriches for response (responders have higher baseline prolactin than non-responders, as in the post-polio pilot — a cohort-level question; at the individual level the test is the engagement readout only); (2) pramipexole titration lowers prolactin in a dose-dependent fashion, and the fall tracks the nausea-threshold dose (@sec:nausea-threshold-dose) until the emetic co-response counteracts it — nausea itself raises prolactin via the stress reflex (@oq:prolactin-buspirone-mechanism), so record nausea at each prolactin draw, and the falsifier is a dose-flat or rising NET pattern across serial draws, not a single flat value. The *direction* of this prediction is already anchored in healthy volunteers (0.5 mg pramipexole lowered prolactin and TSH @Samuels2006PramipexoleModafinil), a single dose in 16 healthy men, so dose-dependence in a fatigue population is the untested part. Falsified if prolactin is dose-flat or rising in net on pramipexole in this population, or shows no responder enrichment.

(Translation gap: post-polio (n = 5) + ME/CFS buspirone challenge (pre-2010) → pramipexole use in ME/CFS. No study has measured prolactin on pramipexole in any fatigue population.)
(Severity applicability: unknown — no severity-stratified data; the test is blood-draw-able at any severity, which is its practical advantage over PET.)

*Consequence:* A routine, inexpensive, repeatable blood test could become the answer to two questions the paper currently cannot answer cheaply — which fatigue patients are worth a dopamine-agonist trial, and whether the drug reached its receptor — in a field where every alternative measurement is a research-only scan.
] <spec:pramipexole-prolactin-dopaminergic-tone-marker>
